# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""NCERT Class 3 Mathematics - Chapter 2: Toy Joy Slide Data"""

slides = [
    {'id': 1, 'title': 'Crafting 3D Shapes', 'badge': 'Creative Geometry', 'sim_id': 'sim_box_bottle_craft', 'description': 'Discover how everyday discarded containers, cardboard boxes, and bottles transform into animal faces while introducing 3D geometric shapes.', 'speech': 'Welcome young mathematicians and creative inventors to our fascinating exploration of solid geometric shapes in Chapter 2, Toy Joy. Look around your home or classroom, and you will notice that our world is brimming with three-dimensional containers. Instead of throwing away empty discarded cartons, tin cans, and plastic bottles, curious young crafters can transform these everyday objects into playful animal and bird faces! Consider a square cardboard carton: by pasting paper ears, button eyes, and a button nose, it turns into an adorable puppy face while embodying the mathematical geometry of a solid cube. Next, take an elongated shoebox: adding a pink snout and floppy ears transforms it into a cheerful pig, demonstrating the rectangular structure of a cuboid. Now examine a round metal soup can or beverage container: painting whiskers and triangular ears creates a charming cat, revealing the curved elegance of a cylinder with two flat circular bases. Finally, rolling stiff chart paper into a pointed party hat crafts the sharp beak of a bird, exemplifying the conical geometry of a cone. Through creative toy crafting, children discover that geometric solids are not distant textbook abstractions, but tangible, functional building blocks that shape the joyful physical world all around us every single day.', 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'Which 3D geometric shape is represented by a round tin can with two flat circular ends?', 'options': ['A cylinder', 'A cube', 'A cone', 'A triangular prism'], 'ans': 'A cylinder', 'explanation': 'A tin can has a curved side surface and two parallel flat circular bases, which defines a cylinder.'}, {'q': 'What geometric shape does a rolled pointed paper party hat resemble?', 'options': ['A cone', 'A cuboid', 'A cylinder', 'A sphere'], 'ans': 'A cone', 'explanation': 'A pointed party hat has a circular flat base tapering smoothly to a sharp top apex, defining a cone.'}]},
    {'id': 2, 'title': 'Anatomy Of 3D', 'badge': 'Structural Features', 'sim_id': 'sim_cube_anatomy', 'description': 'Identify and count the foundational structural parts of solid shapes: flat faces, straight edges, and sharp corners.', 'speech': 'To understand three-dimensional geometry like true mathematical architects, we must investigate the precise anatomical parts that construct every solid object in space. Let us hold a solid wooden cube in our hands and examine its three fundamental anatomical features: faces, edges, and corners, which mathematicians also formally call vertices in higher geometry. First, run your palm smoothly across the flat exterior surfaces of the wooden cube. Each flat surface is called a face; a standard cube possesses exactly six identical square faces: top, bottom, front, back, left, and right. Next, trace your index finger along the boundary lines where two flat faces meet together. These straight boundary lines are called edges; a cube has exactly twelve straight edges of equal length. Finally, gently touch the sharp, pointed tips where three distinct edges intersect together at a single point. These pointed meeting points are called corners or vertices; a cube has exactly eight sharp corners. Remember this essential anatomical triad: six flat faces, twelve straight edges, and eight pointed corners! Recognizing these three foundational geometric properties enables young students to analyze, compare, and construct all varieties of polyhedra and geometric figures across mathematics and architectural engineering with complete scientific confidence.', 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'How many flat faces does a standard solid cube have?', 'options': ['6 flat faces', '8 flat faces', '12 flat faces', '4 flat faces'], 'ans': '6 flat faces', 'explanation': 'A cube has six flat square faces: top, bottom, front, back, left, and right.'}, {'q': 'What is the mathematical name for the sharp pointed tips where three edges meet on a cube?', 'options': ['Corners or vertices', 'Curved surfaces', 'Perimeters', 'Diagonal planes'], 'ans': 'Corners or vertices', 'explanation': 'The sharp points where three edges meet are called corners, or vertices in formal mathematics.'}]},
    {'id': 3, 'title': 'Rocket Shape Assembly', 'badge': 'Spatial Models', 'sim_id': 'sim_rocket_assembly', 'description': 'Analyze a multi-shape space rocket model by identifying, counting, and describing the relative positions of its geometric components.', 'speech': 'One of the most thrilling hands-on activities in our geometric toy laboratory is assembling and analyzing a space rocket model! Imagine building a magnificent toy spacecraft by combining several different three-dimensional blocks together on the classroom table. At the very top of the rocket sits a bright yellow cone, serving as the aerodynamic nose cone that pierces through the air. Directly beneath the cone rests a tall, sturdy blue cylinder, forming the central fuselage body and payload chamber of the rocket. At the base of the rocket sits a solid red cube, anchoring the core engine structure to ensure stability during launch. Attached to the lateral sides of this central core are two slender grey cuboids, functioning as aerodynamic booster fins to maintain flight balance. When young mathematicians inspect this assembled spacecraft, they practice both precise numerical counting and spatial positioning. They record: one cone at the apex, one cylinder in the middle, one cube at the base, and two cuboid stabilizer fins, making a grand total of five geometric components combined into a single unified flying machine! Analyzing how basic geometric blocks stack and lock together reveals how complex real-world aerospace vehicles are designed and engineered.', 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'Which geometric shape is used as the top nose cone of the assembled toy space rocket?', 'options': ['A cone', 'A cylinder', 'A cuboid', 'A sphere'], 'ans': 'A cone', 'explanation': 'The pointed, tapering shape at the apex of the rocket model is a cone.'}, {'q': 'How many total geometric blocks are combined to build the rocket model (1 cone, 1 cylinder, 1 cube, 2 cuboids)?', 'options': ['5 blocks', '4 blocks', '6 blocks', '7 blocks'], 'ans': '5 blocks', 'explanation': 'Adding all components: 1 cone + 1 cylinder + 1 cube + 2 cuboids = 5 geometric blocks.'}]},
    {'id': 4, 'title': 'Toy Engine Components', 'badge': 'Component Counting', 'sim_id': 'sim_toy_engine', 'description': 'Examine a colorful toy railway engine to count and categorize specific component shapes and their functional roles.', 'speech': "Let us now travel to the toy shop to inspect another delightful mechanical marvel: a colorful wooden toy railway engine! A toy locomotive is an intricate composite structure assembled from diverse geometric shapes painted in bright primary colors. When young observers analyze the train engine carefully, they can decompose its complex body into distinct geometric parts. The main steam boiler is constructed from a long, smooth red cylinder that lies horizontally along the chassis. Perched atop the boiler is a small yellow cone that functions as the puffing smokestack chimney. Behind the boiler sits a tall grey cuboid that forms the sheltered driver's cabin, complete with rectangular window cutouts. Beneath the cabin rests a solid blue cube that serves as the sturdy coal tender storage box. Finally, supporting the entire locomotive chassis are six small, flat black cylinders that rotate smoothly as train wheels on the railway track. By systematically sorting and tallying these parts, students count one horizontal cylinder, one cone smokestack, one cuboid driver cabin, one cube tender, and six wheel cylinders. Overall, carefully analyzing toy train components trains young inquisitive observers to recognize elementary geometric solids inside complex everyday machinery and real-world vehicles.", 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'What 3D geometric shape forms the rotating wheels of the toy railway locomotive?', 'options': ['Flat circular cylinders', 'Sharp square cubes', 'Pointed cones', 'Rectangular cuboids'], 'ans': 'Flat circular cylinders', 'explanation': 'Wheels are circular discs with depth, which mathematically represents short flat cylinders.'}, {'q': "Which shape serves as the tall driver's cabin on the toy train engine?", 'options': ['A grey cuboid', 'A round sphere', 'A yellow cone', 'A wooden pyramid'], 'ans': 'A grey cuboid', 'explanation': "The driver's cabin is constructed from an upright rectangular box, representing a cuboid."}]},
    {'id': 5, 'title': 'Cube Versus Cuboid', 'badge': 'Special Relationship', 'sim_id': 'sim_cube_is_cuboid', 'description': 'Explore the foundational geometric principle that reveals why every cube is a special, perfectly balanced type of cuboid.', 'speech': 'One of the most profound and elegant discoveries in primary school geometry is the special relationship between cubes and cuboids. Many young learners initially think that a cube and a cuboid are completely unrelated shapes. However, careful mathematical analysis reveals a beautiful truth: a cube is actually a special type of cuboid! Let us investigate why this is true by examining their shared geometric definitions. Both a cube and a cuboid have exactly six flat faces, twelve straight edges, and eight sharp corners. In a general cuboid, such as a rectangular cereal box or a textbook, opposite faces are equal rectangles. The length, width, and height of a general cuboid can all have completely different measurements. Now, imagine shrinking the longer length and expanding the shorter width until all three dimensions become perfectly equal: length equals width, which equals height! When this happens, every single one of the six rectangular faces becomes an identical square. The shape is now called a cube! Because a square is a special type of rectangle, a cube satisfies every single rule required of a cuboid. Understanding that every cube is a cuboid—but not every cuboid is a cube—deepens mathematical classification skills.', 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'Why is a cube considered a special type of cuboid?', 'options': ['All of its six rectangular faces are equal squares with identical lengths', 'It has completely round curved edges with no corners', 'It can roll like a ball across a flat surface', 'It has only three faces instead of six'], 'ans': 'All of its six rectangular faces are equal squares with identical lengths', 'explanation': 'A cube satisfies all cuboid rules, with the special property that length = width = height, making all 6 faces squares.'}, {'q': 'Is every cuboid also a cube?', 'options': ['No, a cuboid is only a cube when all its faces are equal squares', 'Yes, every single rectangular box is always an exact cube', 'Yes, all geometric shapes are cubes', 'No, cuboids have zero flat faces'], 'ans': 'No, a cuboid is only a cube when all its faces are equal squares', 'explanation': 'A general cuboid can have unequal rectangular faces; it only becomes a cube when all sides are equal.'}]},
    {'id': 6, 'title': 'Flat Curved Faces', 'badge': 'Surface Properties', 'sim_id': 'sim_flat_curved_surfaces', 'description': 'Distinguish between flat faces that slide smoothly and curved surfaces that roll continuously across inclined surfaces.', 'speech': 'When solid geometric shapes interact with flat surfaces or ramps, their physical behavior is determined entirely by surface curvature! Solid shapes possess two fundamentally different kinds of surfaces: flat faces and curved surfaces. A flat face is completely plane, level, and straight, like the cover of a hardcover book or the tabletop. When you push an object with flat faces—such as a wooden cube or a rectangular matchbox—down an inclined wooden ramp, it slides smoothly along its base without rotating. In sharp contrast, a curved surface is continuously rounded and bent, like the outer skin of an orange or a cricket ball. When you place a sphere on a ramp, it rolls rapidly down the slope because it has zero flat faces and zero straight edges! Even more fascinating are shapes that possess both types of surfaces simultaneously. Consider a cylinder: its rounded side allows it to roll smoothly like a rolling pin, yet its two flat circular ends allow it to slide or stack securely like a coin! Similarly, a cone rolls in circular loops on its curved flank, but stands upright on its flat circular base. Observing rolling and sliding reveals how surface geometry governs mechanical motion in physical toys.', 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'Which of the following geometric shapes has both flat circular faces and a curved surface?', 'options': ['A cylinder', 'A cube', 'A sphere', 'A cuboid'], 'ans': 'A cylinder', 'explanation': 'A cylinder has two flat circular faces (top and bottom) and one smoothly curved side surface.'}, {'q': 'Why does a sphere roll effortlessly down an inclined ramp in any direction?', 'options': ['It has a continuously curved surface with zero flat faces and zero edges', 'It is filled with magnetic liquid inside', 'It has eight sharp corners that grip the ground', 'It is made of light transparent paper'], 'ans': 'It has a continuously curved surface with zero flat faces and zero edges', 'explanation': 'A sphere consists solely of a continuous curved surface, allowing it to roll smoothly in all directions.'}]},
    {'id': 7, 'title': 'Shape Mystery Descriptions', 'badge': 'Spatial Communication', 'sim_id': 'sim_mystery_shape_describer', 'description': 'Play a collaborative spatial construction game where precise verbal descriptions enable partners to duplicate models blindly.', 'speech': "Mathematics is not only about numbers and formulas; it is also a powerful language of precise spatial communication! In Chapter 2, students engage in an exhilarating classroom challenge called the Collaborative Construction Game. In this game, two student partners sit back-to-back so they cannot see each other's desk. The first student, acting as the architect, selects three geometric shapes and builds a secret multi-tier structure behind a cardboard screen. For example, the architect places a wide wooden cuboid flat on the table, stacks a wooden cylinder vertically on top of it, and crowns the cylinder with a pointed yellow cone. Now comes the true mathematical challenge: the architect must verbally describe this construction to the partner using clear geometric terminology and precise spatial position words! The architect says: 'First, lay a rectangular cuboid flat as the foundation. Next, place a cylinder upright on the center of the cuboid's top face. Finally, balance a cone on top of the cylinder with its sharp apex pointing upward.' By listening carefully and interpreting spatial terms like 'base', 'center', and 'apex', the partner replicates the exact structure without ever seeing the original model! This activity proves how clear mathematical vocabulary enables flawless cooperative problem-solving.", 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'What is the primary skill developed by playing the back-to-back shape construction game?', 'options': ['Precise spatial vocabulary and verbal geometric communication', 'Running faster during outdoor sports races', 'Memorizing long lists of multiplication tables', 'Painting pictures with watercolor paints'], 'ans': 'Precise spatial vocabulary and verbal geometric communication', 'explanation': 'The game trains students to describe 3D positions and shapes clearly so partners can replicate them sight unseen.'}, {'q': 'If a cone is balanced on top of a cylinder with its sharp tip facing the ceiling, what is the tip called?', 'options': ['The apex or top corner', 'The circular base', 'The horizontal perimeter', 'The rectangular side'], 'ans': 'The apex or top corner', 'explanation': 'The pointed sharp tip of a cone opposite its circular base is called the apex.'}]},
    {'id': 8, 'title': 'Classroom Shape Hunt', 'badge': 'Environmental Data', 'sim_id': 'sim_classroom_shape_hunt', 'description': 'Embark on an interactive shape safari around the classroom to discover and record real-world 3D geometric figures.', 'speech': "Armed with our knowledge of solid geometric anatomy, let us now embark on an exciting Classroom Shape Hunt! Every classroom is an extraordinary treasure trove of geometric forms waiting to be discovered by observant young detectives. Carrying tally clipboards, students survey the room to identify items that correspond to our core 3D shapes: cubes, cuboids, cylinders, cones, and spheres. Look at the teacher's desk: a rectangular chalk box and thick dictionary represent cuboids. Inspect the writing board: colorful spherical marbles and a world globe on the bookshelf represent perfect spheres. Glance toward the stationery stand: wooden pencils, glue sticks, and cylindrical drinking water bottles display round cylindrical barrels. Notice the party decorations or sharpened pencil tips: pointed cones catch our eye. Students also discover fascinating composite objects constructed from multiple shapes: a flashlight combines a cylindrical handle with a conical lamp head; a sharpened pencil combines a hexagonal cylinder body with a conical graphite point! By tallying their discoveries, students record which shapes appear most frequently—usually cuboids and cylinders—and which appear least often. Conducting an active classroom shape hunt demonstrates vividly that solid geometry is alive, useful, and thriving in every corner of our daily environment.", 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'Which 3D shape is typically represented by a standard classroom world globe?', 'options': ['A sphere', 'A cylinder', 'A cube', 'A cone'], 'ans': 'A sphere', 'explanation': 'A world globe is a completely round, symmetrical 3D ball, which is a sphere.'}, {'q': 'What two 3D shapes combine to form a newly sharpened wooden pencil?', 'options': ['A cylinder (body) and a cone (sharpened tip)', 'A cube and a sphere', 'Two cuboids stacked together', 'A pyramid and a sphere'], 'ans': 'A cylinder (body) and a cone (sharpened tip)', 'explanation': 'The main wooden shaft of a pencil is a cylinder, while the sharpened writing tip forms a cone.'}]},
    {'id': 9, 'title': 'Sorting Solid Shapes', 'badge': 'Property Sorting', 'sim_id': 'sim_shape_property_sorter', 'description': 'Classify diverse solid shapes into distinct categories based on their dynamic rolling, sliding, and stacking capabilities.', 'speech': 'Now that we have discovered various geometric solids, let us organize them through systematic mathematical classification! In this sorting investigation, solid shapes are grouped into three distinct categories based on how they move across flat surfaces: shapes that only roll, shapes that only slide, and versatile shapes that can both roll and slide! Objects with exclusively curved surfaces, like a round cricket ball or a smooth glass marble, belong to the rolling group. Because a sphere lacks flat faces, it rolls effortlessly in any direction but cannot slide or stack securely. Conversely, objects bounded purely by flat polygonal faces—such as wooden cubes, rectangular gift boxes, and geometric cuboids—belong to the sliding group. When pushed, they glide steadily across table surfaces without rotating, and their flat horizontal tops allow multiple boxes to stack into tall, stable towers! Finally, cylinders and cones form the extraordinary dual-action category. Place a cylindrical tin on its round curved side, and it rolls briskly down the ramp; place it upright on its flat circular face, and it slides smoothly or acts as a sturdy building pedestal! Sorting shapes by mechanical behavior helps us select optimal structural components when designing real engineering marvels.', 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'Which group of solid shapes can both roll and slide depending on how they are positioned?', 'options': ['Cylinders and cones', 'Cubes and cuboids', 'Only spherical balls', 'Only flat square tiles'], 'ans': 'Cylinders and cones', 'explanation': 'Cylinders and cones have flat circular faces for sliding and curved lateral surfaces for rolling.'}, {'q': 'Why can multiple rectangular cuboids and cubes be easily stacked into tall, sturdy towers?', 'options': ['They possess flat planar faces that rest firmly against one another', 'They have magnetic curved sides that attract each other', 'They are lighter than air and float upwards', 'They possess zero straight edges or corners'], 'ans': 'They possess flat planar faces that rest firmly against one another', 'explanation': 'Flat faces provide stable, level contact areas that prevent stacked blocks from toppling over.'}]},
    {'id': 10, 'title': 'Clay Stick Skeletons', 'badge': 'Skeleton Frames', 'sim_id': 'sim_clay_stick_builder', 'description': 'Construct hollow wireframe skeleton models of 3D solids using small clay balls for corners and wooden sticks for edges.', 'speech': 'To truly master the internal geometry of solid figures, nothing equals the joy of building three-dimensional skeleton frames! In this tactile craftwork laboratory, we assemble hollow geometric skeletons using two simple materials: small molded clay balls and straight wooden matchsticks or toothpicks. This hands-on modeling reveals an essential structural correspondence in solid geometry: every straight wooden stick represents an edge, and every moist clay ball that joins sticks together represents a corner or vertex! To construct the skeleton frame of a cube, we first roll eight equal spherical balls of soft modeling clay. Next, we take twelve straight matchsticks of identical length. By inserting four sticks into four clay balls, we build a flat square base. We erect four vertical sticks from these corners, and finally cap them with four more sticks and four top clay balls. The completed open cube stands firm, displaying its eight clay corners and twelve straight stick edges in clear three-dimensional space! If we build a rectangular cuboid instead, we still require exactly eight clay corner balls and twelve straight boundary sticks, though the sticks must come in three differing length sets for length, breadth, and height. Crafting these skeleton models provides an unforgettable physical demonstration of mathematical vertices, edges, and internal spatial architecture.', 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'In a clay and matchstick skeleton model of a cube, what do the clay balls represent?', 'options': ['The sharp corners or vertices', 'The flat exterior faces', 'The straight boundary edges', 'The hollow internal volume'], 'ans': 'The sharp corners or vertices', 'explanation': 'Clay balls connect intersecting sticks, physically representing the vertices or corners of the shape.'}, {'q': 'How many matchsticks and clay balls are needed to build the complete skeleton frame of a cuboid?', 'options': ['12 matchsticks (edges) and 8 clay balls (vertices)', '6 matchsticks and 6 clay balls', '8 matchsticks and 12 clay balls', '10 matchsticks and 5 clay balls'], 'ans': '12 matchsticks (edges) and 8 clay balls (vertices)', 'explanation': 'A cuboid has 12 straight edges (matchsticks) meeting at 8 corners (clay balls).'}]},
    {'id': 11, 'title': 'Toy Joy Board', 'badge': 'Mathematical Journey', 'sim_id': 'sim_toy_joy_board_game', 'description': 'Navigate a colorful geometric board game where rolling dice and identifying shape attributes advance player tokens.', 'speech': "Welcome to the vibrant Toy Joy Board Game, an interactive mathematical journey where geometric mastery leads to victory! The board is laid out as a meandering winding pathway of numbered tiles, each marked with colorful geometric solid illustrations. Players begin at the toy workshop starting gate and take turns casting a six-sided dotted wooden cube: the game die! As tokens advance along the path, players land on diverse property checkpoint squares that challenge their geometric reasoning. For instance, stepping onto a 'Cylinder Square' prompts the player to state how many flat faces a water bottle possesses. Landing on an 'Edge Bridge' asks the player to count the twelve edges of a wooden storage chest to earn a forward boost! Special bonus tiles reward students who can spot real-life shapes: identify a cone on a celebration banner to skip ahead three spaces, or name a spherical sport ball to take an express shortcut! However, landing on a 'Curved Slip' requires demonstrating why a rolling sphere cannot stack, sliding the token back one space. By weaving core geometric definitions directly into thrilling board game rules, mathematics transforms into an engaging, interactive adventure for all young learners. Every turn reinforces shape properties, edge counting, and spatial orientation through joyful friendly competition.", 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'What geometric shape is the dotted playing die used in the Toy Joy board game?', 'options': ['A symmetrical cube', 'A long cylinder', 'A pointed cone', 'A flat triangle'], 'ans': 'A symmetrical cube', 'explanation': 'A standard game die is a symmetrical six-faced solid cube.'}, {'q': 'If a board tile asks how many flat faces a solid cylinder possesses, what is the correct answer?', 'options': ['2 flat circular faces', '6 flat rectangular faces', '0 flat faces', '4 triangular faces'], 'ans': '2 flat circular faces', 'explanation': 'A cylinder has 2 flat parallel circular faces (top and bottom) joined by one curved surface.'}]},
    {'id': 12, 'title': 'Standard Dice Dots', 'badge': 'Opposite Sum Rule', 'sim_id': 'sim_dice_opposite_faces', 'description': 'Uncover the universal arithmetic secret of standard dot dice: opposite faces always add up to exactly seven.', 'speech': "Take a close look at a standard game die, and you will uncover one of arithmetic's most delightful hidden secrets! A standard die is a cube whose six square faces are marked with small black dots called pips, representing numbers from one to six. At first glance, the placement of these dots might appear completely random. However, dice makers throughout centuries have followed a strict mathematical convention: the numbers on opposite faces of a standard die always add up to exactly seven! Let us verify this universal opposite sum rule by pairing each face with the face directly on the reverse side. The face with one dot is positioned directly opposite the face with six dots: one plus six equals seven! The face showing two dots sits directly opposite the face showing five dots: two plus five equals seven! Finally, the face showing three dots is positioned opposite the face showing four dots: three plus four equals seven! This elegant rule means that if you know the number showing on the top face of a standard die, you can instantly deduce the hidden number on the bottom face by subtracting from seven! Exploring dice patterns bridges spatial geometry with rapid mental arithmetic.", 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'According to the standard dice rule, what is the constant sum of numbers on opposite faces?', 'options': ['7', '6', '10', '12'], 'ans': '7', 'explanation': 'Opposite faces on any standard die always sum to 7: 1+6=7, 2+5=7, and 3+4=7.'}, {'q': 'If a standard die resting on a table shows 5 dots on its top face, how many dots are on the hidden bottom face?', 'options': ['2 dots', '1 dot', '3 dots', '4 dots'], 'ans': '2 dots', 'explanation': 'Because opposite faces add up to 7, the bottom face has 7 - 5 = 2 dots.'}]},
    {'id': 13, 'title': 'Joining Identical Cubes', 'badge': 'Composite Cuboid', 'sim_id': 'sim_two_cubes_join', 'description': 'Discover what happens when two identical wooden cubes are joined face-to-face to create a brand new composite cuboid.', 'speech': 'What fascinating new geometric structure emerges when two identical wooden cubes are glued together along their square faces? Let us perform this classic geometric experiment step by step to observe how dimensions and surface properties transform. Begin with two separate wooden cubes of identical side length, say five centimeters each. Separately, each cube has six flat square faces, giving twelve individual faces in total. Now, press one square face of the first cube against a matching square face of the second cube. When these two faces touch, they become glued together inside the seam and disappear from the exterior surface! The two joined faces are no longer exposed to the outside. What shape have we constructed? The resulting combined solid is no longer a cube because its length has doubled to ten centimeters, while its width and height remain five centimeters! Because its three dimensions are no longer all equal, the new single solid is a rectangular cuboid! Looking at the exterior of this composite cuboid, we count six outer faces: two square ends and four elongated rectangular side faces. Joining modular blocks clearly illustrates how simple elementary units combine into sophisticated three-dimensional solid geometric architectures throughout design and engineering.', 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'When two identical cubes are joined side-by-side along one face, what new solid geometric shape is formed?', 'options': ['A rectangular cuboid', 'A larger single cube', 'A round cylinder', 'A pointed cone'], 'ans': 'A rectangular cuboid', 'explanation': 'The length doubles while width and height stay the same, creating a cuboid with unequal side dimensions.'}, {'q': 'How many individual square faces become hidden inside the contact seam when two cubes are glued together?', 'options': ['2 square faces (one from each cube)', '4 square faces', '1 square face', '6 square faces'], 'ans': '2 square faces (one from each cube)', 'explanation': 'The two touching faces meet inside the seam, hiding exactly 2 faces from the outside.'}]},
    {'id': 14, 'title': 'Three Cube Arrangements', 'badge': 'Spatial Configurations', 'sim_id': 'sim_three_cube_variations', 'description': 'Explore and compare the different spatial configurations possible when connecting three identical unit cubes.', 'speech': 'Building upon our twin-cube experiment, let us now investigate the intriguing possibilities of connecting three identical unit cubes! When we have three unit cubes, how many distinctly different spatial structures can we assemble? The first and most direct configuration is to join all three cubes in a straight, continuous line. In this linear arrangement, the cubes form an elongated rectangular cuboid whose length is three times the unit width. Here, two internal contact seams hide four square faces, leaving fourteen unit squares exposed on the outer surface. Now, let us rearrange the blocks! Instead of a straight line, attach the third cube to the side of the second cube at a right angle. This creates an eye-catching L-shaped composite figure, often called a corner block or triomino solid! Notice something remarkable about this L-shaped block: although it still uses exactly three unit cubes and hides four interior faces, it is no longer a simple rectangular cuboid! It has indented inner corners and protruding arms. Comparing the straight cuboid with the bent L-shape teaches students that identical volume can yield entirely different geometric shapes. Spatial reasoning flourishes as children visualize rotations and rearrangements of multi-cube assemblies.', 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'When three identical cubes are placed in a single straight row, what geometric shape is created?', 'options': ['An elongated cuboid', 'A perfect single cube', 'A round sphere', 'A triangular pyramid'], 'ans': 'An elongated cuboid', 'explanation': 'Connecting three cubes in a straight line produces an elongated box shape, which is a rectangular cuboid.'}, {'q': 'How many total unit cubes are used in making an L-shaped corner arrangement of three blocks?', 'options': ['3 cubes', '4 cubes', '6 cubes', '2 cubes'], 'ans': '3 cubes', 'explanation': 'Both the straight bar and the L-shaped corner are constructed using exactly 3 identical unit cubes.'}]},
    {'id': 15, 'title': 'Composite Model Stacks', 'badge': 'Hidden Block Counting', 'sim_id': 'sim_cube_block_counter', 'description': 'Master 3D spatial visualization by counting visible and hidden unit cubes in complex stacked block towers and staircases.', 'speech': 'One of the most thrilling challenges in three-dimensional geometry is counting unit cubes in multi-layer composite structures! When children build steps, towers, or miniature castles from wooden blocks, some blocks remain clearly visible from the front, while other blocks are hidden underneath upper tiers to support the weight above. Consider a three-step wooden staircase. The bottom front step consists of one cube resting on the table. The middle step stands two cubes high. The top back step rises three cubes high. If an untrained viewer only glances from the front, they might mistakenly count only the top visible surfaces: one plus one plus one! However, a thoughtful mathematician understands gravity and structural support: a block cannot float in empty mid-air! To support the top step at height three, there must be a vertical column of three cubes resting on the ground. Under the second step, there must be a column of two cubes. Under the first step, there is one single foundation cube. Adding column heights systematically—one plus two plus three—reveals that the staircase contains exactly six unit cubes! Mastering hidden cube deduction transforms flat two-dimensional textbook drawings into vibrant three-dimensional spatial reality in young mathematical minds.', 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'In a 3-step staircase with columns of heights 1, 2, and 3 cubes, how many total unit cubes are used?', 'options': ['6 cubes (1 + 2 + 3 = 6)', '3 cubes', '5 cubes', '9 cubes'], 'ans': '6 cubes (1 + 2 + 3 = 6)', 'explanation': 'Summing the vertical cube columns: 1 + 2 + 3 = 6 unit cubes in total.'}, {'q': 'Why must there be hidden blocks beneath the elevated top tier of a stacked block tower?', 'options': ['Blocks cannot float in mid-air and need solid support cubes underneath', 'The wind blows them away otherwise', 'Because all cubes are transparent like glass', 'Because top cubes are twice as heavy'], 'ans': 'Blocks cannot float in mid-air and need solid support cubes underneath', 'explanation': "Physical structures require supporting blocks below elevated tiers so they don't fall."}]},
    {'id': 16, 'title': 'Master Geometry Matrix', 'badge': 'Comprehensive Synthesis', 'sim_id': 'sim_master_geometry_table', 'description': 'Synthesize all solid geometric shapes across faces, edges, vertices, and mechanical behaviors in a master comparison matrix.', 'speech': 'Congratulations on journeying through the wonderland of three-dimensional shapes in Chapter 2, Toy Joy! To consolidate our geometric mastery, let us unite all our discoveries into one grand Master Geometry Matrix. This comprehensive synthesis table compares our five core solids across every critical mathematical property. First, the cube: six equal square faces, twelve straight edges, eight sharp corners; it slides and stacks perfectly! Second, the cuboid: six rectangular faces, twelve straight edges, eight corners; it slides smoothly and stacks with great stability. Third, the cylinder: two flat circular faces, one curved surface, two curved edges, zero corners; it can roll, slide, and stack! Fourth, the cone: one flat circular base, one curved surface, one circular edge, and one pointed apex; it rolls in arcs and slides on its base. Fifth, the sphere: zero flat faces, one continuous curved surface, zero edges, and zero corners; it rolls smoothly in every direction! Notice the remarkable mathematical harmony: as flat faces give way to curved surfaces, sliding behavior transitions into rotational rolling! Every single three-dimensional solid around us follows these universal geometric principles of faces, edges, and corners. With this master matrix at your command, you possess the geometric insight to analyze and build toys, buildings, and machines throughout our physical world.', 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'Which 3D solid possesses 2 flat circular faces, 1 curved surface, 2 curved edges, and 0 corners?', 'options': ['A cylinder', 'A sphere', 'A cube', 'A cuboid'], 'ans': 'A cylinder', 'explanation': 'A cylinder has 2 flat circular bases, 1 curved lateral surface, 2 curved rims, and zero corners.'}, {'q': 'How many flat faces and corners does a perfect sphere possess?', 'options': ['0 flat faces and 0 corners', '1 flat face and 1 corner', '6 flat faces and 8 corners', '2 flat faces and 0 corners'], 'ans': '0 flat faces and 0 corners', 'explanation': 'A sphere is completely curved all around; it has zero flat faces, zero edges, and zero corners.'}]},
    {'id': 17, 'title': 'Challenge 1', 'badge': 'Shape Identification', 'description': 'Test your ability to identify 3D solid shapes, recognize real-world objects, and analyze toy assemblies.', 'questions': [{'id': 'c1_q1', 'q': 'Which 3D solid shape does a cylindrical drinking can resemble?', 'options': ['A cylinder', 'A cube', 'A sphere', 'A cone'], 'ans': 'A cylinder', 'hint': 'Think about a shape with two circular flat ends and a smooth curved side.', 'explanation': 'A drinking can has two identical parallel circular faces and a curved wall, making it a cylinder.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Observe the physical characteristics of a metal beverage can.', 'step2': 'Identify its two circular flat ends and rounded lateral surface.', 'step3': 'Conclude that this matches the geometric definition of a cylinder.'}}, {'id': 'c1_q2', 'q': 'What 3D shape is formed by a sharp birthday party hat?', 'options': ['A cone', 'A cylinder', 'A cuboid', 'A sphere'], 'ans': 'A cone', 'hint': 'It has one circular flat base and tapers up to a sharp pointed tip.', 'explanation': 'A party hat has a circular opening at the base and tapers to a single pointed vertex (apex), which defines a cone.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Examine the outline of a celebratory party hat.', 'step2': 'Note that it narrows smoothly to a sharp point at the top.', 'step3': 'Recognize that a 3D solid with one circular base and a pointed apex is a cone.'}}, {'id': 'c1_q3', 'q': 'In a toy rocket model, which shape is placed at the very top as the nose cone?', 'options': ['A cone', 'A cube', 'A cylinder', 'A sphere'], 'ans': 'A cone', 'hint': 'The aerodynamic tip of a rocket is pointed to pierce through the air.', 'explanation': 'The aerodynamic nose of a toy rocket is shaped like a cone to point upwards.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Recall the structural assembly of the toy rocket.', 'step2': 'Identify the topmost pointed component.', 'step3': 'Confirm that a pointed solid with a circular base is a cone.'}}, {'id': 'c1_q4', 'q': 'In a wooden toy locomotive, which 3D shape is used to represent the horizontal steam boiler?', 'options': ['A long cylinder', 'A flat square cube', 'A pointed cone', 'A round sphere'], 'ans': 'A long cylinder', 'hint': 'Boilers on steam trains are cylindrical pressurized tanks.', 'explanation': 'The long, rounded central body of a steam train locomotive is modeled using a horizontal cylinder.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Look at the parts making up the toy railway locomotive.', 'step2': 'Locate the long red central body holding steam.', 'step3': 'Conclude that this horizontally placed tube is a cylinder.'}}, {'id': 'c1_q5', 'q': 'A rectangular shoe box or cereal carton is an everyday example of which 3D solid?', 'options': ['A cuboid', 'A sphere', 'A cone', 'A cylinder'], 'ans': 'A cuboid', 'hint': 'It has six flat rectangular faces with length, width, and height.', 'explanation': 'A box with six rectangular faces where opposite faces are equal is a rectangular cuboid.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Analyze the faces of a standard shoe box.', 'step2': 'Observe that all 6 faces are rectangles meeting at right angles.', 'step3': 'Classify this shape as a cuboid.'}}, {'id': 'c1_q6', 'q': 'Which toy train component is typically shaped as a cone?', 'options': ['The puffing smokestack chimney', 'The flat railway tracks', "The rectangular driver's cabin", 'The circular wheels'], 'ans': 'The puffing smokestack chimney', 'hint': 'It sits upright on top of the boiler to let steam escape.', 'explanation': 'The smokestack atop the boiler tapers upward, which is modeled as a small yellow cone.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Identify the chimney pipe on the toy train.', 'step2': 'Observe that it is shaped like an inverted or upright cone.', 'step3': 'Match it with the smokestack component.'}}, {'id': 'c1_q7', 'q': 'Which everyday classroom item has the exact 3D geometric shape of a sphere?', 'options': ['A world globe', 'A chalkboard duster', 'A flat notebook', 'A triangular ruler'], 'ans': 'A world globe', 'hint': 'It is completely round and can roll in all directions.', 'explanation': 'A world globe is completely round and symmetrical from every angle, representing a sphere.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Check the geometry of each listed classroom object.', 'step2': 'A duster is a cuboid; a notebook is flat; a globe is a 3D ball.', 'step3': 'Confirm that a globe represents a sphere.'}}, {'id': 'c1_q8', 'q': 'If a toy rocket body has 1 cone top, 1 cylinder fuselage, and 1 cuboid launch base, how many total 3D parts are used?', 'options': ['3 parts', '5 parts', '2 parts', '4 parts'], 'ans': '3 parts', 'hint': 'Add the counts: 1 + 1 + 1.', 'explanation': '1 cone + 1 cylinder + 1 cuboid = 3 total parts.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Count each shape: 1 cone, 1 cylinder, 1 cuboid.', 'step2': 'Calculate 1 + 1 + 1 = 3.', 'step3': 'Select 3 parts as the total count.'}}, {'id': 'c1_q9', 'q': 'What 3D shape is formed by six equal square wooden blocks glued together to form a solid box?', 'options': ['A cube', 'A sphere', 'A cylinder', 'A cone'], 'ans': 'A cube', 'hint': 'All six faces are identical squares.', 'explanation': 'A solid with six identical square faces is a cube.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Note that all 6 faces are equal squares.', 'step2': 'Recall that when length = width = height, a cuboid is a cube.', 'step3': 'Conclude the shape is a cube.'}}, {'id': 'c1_q10', 'q': 'Which part of a newly sharpened pencil resembles a cone?', 'options': ['The sharpened writing tip', 'The long wooden barrel', 'The flat circular eraser base', 'The hexagonal grip'], 'ans': 'The sharpened writing tip', 'hint': 'The tip is shaved down to a sharp point for writing.', 'explanation': 'The shaved writing point of a pencil tapers to a vertex, forming a conical tip.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Inspect a sharpened pencil from eraser to tip.', 'step2': 'The wooden shaft is a cylinder/prism, while the tip tapers to a point.', 'step3': 'Identify the sharpened point as a cone.'}}]},
    {'id': 18, 'title': 'Challenge 2', 'badge': 'Anatomy & Motion', 'description': 'Demonstrate your understanding of faces, edges, corners, and the dynamic laws of rolling and sliding.', 'questions': [{'id': 'c2_q1', 'q': 'How many flat faces, straight edges, and sharp corners does a standard cube have?', 'options': ['6 faces, 12 edges, 8 corners', '8 faces, 12 edges, 6 corners', '6 faces, 8 edges, 12 corners', '4 faces, 6 edges, 4 corners'], 'ans': '6 faces, 12 edges, 8 corners', 'hint': 'A cube has 6 square faces, 12 boundary edges, and 8 corner points.', 'explanation': 'A cube has 6 flat square faces, 12 straight edges, and 8 vertices (corners).', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Count the flat square faces of a cube (6).', 'step2': 'Count the straight edges where faces meet (12).', 'step3': 'Count the corner points where edges meet (8).'}}, {'id': 'c2_q2', 'q': 'How many corners (vertices) does a solid sphere have?', 'options': ['0 corners', '1 corner', '4 corners', '8 corners'], 'ans': '0 corners', 'hint': 'A sphere is completely round with no sharp points anywhere.', 'explanation': 'A sphere consists of a continuous curved surface with zero flat faces, zero edges, and zero corners.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Recall the geometric definition of a vertex (a sharp point where edges meet).', 'step2': 'Observe that a sphere has no edges or flat planes.', 'step3': 'Conclude that a sphere has 0 corners.'}}, {'id': 'c2_q3', 'q': 'How many circular edges does a solid cylinder possess?', 'options': ['2 circular edges', '0 edges', '4 straight edges', '1 circular edge'], 'ans': '2 circular edges', 'hint': 'Count the rims where the top and bottom circular faces meet the curved side.', 'explanation': 'A cylinder has 2 circular edges where its top and bottom flat faces meet the curved body.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Look at where the flat circular ends join the curved cylindrical side.', 'step2': 'Each end creates one curved boundary rim.', 'step3': 'Total curved edges = 2.'}}, {'id': 'c2_q4', 'q': 'Why does a marble roll down a ramp while a wooden block slides?', 'options': ['A marble has a curved surface, while a block has flat faces', 'A marble is heavier than a wooden block', 'A block has wheels hidden underneath', 'A marble is made of liquid'], 'ans': 'A marble has a curved surface, while a block has flat faces', 'hint': 'Curved surfaces roll continuously; flat faces slide along the plane.', 'explanation': 'Curved surfaces turn smoothly over a slope to roll, whereas flat surfaces slide along the incline.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Compare the surfaces of a marble (curved) and a block (flat).', 'step2': 'Curved surfaces rotate continuously during motion (rolling).', 'step3': 'Flat surfaces stay in planar contact while sliding.'}}, {'id': 'c2_q5', 'q': 'Which of these 3D shapes can both roll and slide?', 'options': ['A cylinder', 'A cube', 'A cuboid', 'A world globe'], 'ans': 'A cylinder', 'hint': 'It can roll on its curved side and slide on its flat circular base.', 'explanation': 'A cylinder rolls when placed on its curved wall and slides when placed on either flat circular face.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Identify shapes possessing both flat faces and curved surfaces.', 'step2': 'A cylinder has 2 flat faces (slides) and 1 curved surface (rolls).', 'step3': 'Confirm that a cylinder can both roll and slide.'}}, {'id': 'c2_q6', 'q': 'What is the sharp pointed top of a cone called?', 'options': ['The apex or top corner', 'The perimeter', 'The flat diameter', 'The curved edge'], 'ans': 'The apex or top corner', 'hint': 'It is the single sharp point at the pinnacle of the cone.', 'explanation': 'The single vertex at the tip of a cone opposite its circular base is called the apex.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Examine the components of a cone: 1 circular base, 1 curved surface, 1 vertex.', 'step2': 'The vertex at the pointed tip opposite the base is the apex.', 'step3': "Select 'The apex or top corner'."}}, {'id': 'c2_q7', 'q': 'How many flat faces does a solid cone have?', 'options': ['1 flat circular face', '2 flat faces', '0 flat faces', '3 triangular faces'], 'ans': '1 flat circular face', 'hint': 'Check the bottom base of the cone.', 'explanation': 'A cone has exactly 1 flat face, which is its circular base.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Analyze the surface parts of a cone.', 'step2': 'The base is a flat circle; the remaining body is a curved lateral surface.', 'step3': 'Conclude that it has exactly 1 flat face.'}}, {'id': 'c2_q8', 'q': 'Which statement correctly describes the relationship between a cube and a cuboid?', 'options': ['Every cube is a special cuboid with all equal square faces', 'Every cuboid is always a cube', 'Cubes and cuboids have completely different numbers of corners', 'A cube has no flat faces'], 'ans': 'Every cube is a special cuboid with all equal square faces', 'hint': 'A cube satisfies all cuboid rules, with the extra condition that all sides are equal.', 'explanation': 'A cube is a special cuboid where length, width, and height are identical, making all six faces equal squares.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Recall the definitions of cube and cuboid (both have 6 faces, 12 edges, 8 vertices).', 'step2': 'A square is a special rectangle; thus, equal-sided boxes are special cuboids.', 'step3': 'Conclude that every cube is a cuboid.'}}, {'id': 'c2_q9', 'q': 'Why can you NOT stack spheres on top of each other to make a tall stable tower?', 'options': ['Spheres have zero flat faces to rest stably upon', 'Spheres are too soft and squash flat', 'Spheres have sharp corners that push them apart', 'Spheres are magnetic and repel each other'], 'ans': 'Spheres have zero flat faces to rest stably upon', 'hint': 'Stacking requires flat surfaces to distribute weight without rolling off.', 'explanation': 'Because spheres have only a curved surface with zero flat faces, they roll off each other when stacked without support.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Consider what makes stacked objects stable: flat horizontal contact planes.', 'step2': 'Observe that spheres touch only at a single point and easily roll.', 'step3': 'Conclude that the lack of flat faces prevents stacking.'}}, {'id': 'c2_q10', 'q': 'What is the line where two flat faces of a solid meet called?', 'options': ['An edge', 'A vertex', 'A perimeter circle', 'A diagonal'], 'ans': 'An edge', 'hint': 'It is the straight boundary segment joining two adjacent faces.', 'explanation': 'An edge is the line segment where two faces of a three-dimensional solid intersect.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Recall the anatomical definitions of solid geometry.', 'step2': 'Faces are flat surfaces; edges are lines where faces meet; corners are points where edges meet.', 'step3': "Select 'An edge'."}}]},
    {'id': 19, 'title': 'Challenge 3', 'badge': 'Frames & Dice Rules', 'description': 'Master wireframe skeleton construction with clay balls and sticks, and apply the opposite-sum rule of standard dice.', 'questions': [{'id': 'c3_q1', 'q': 'When building a skeleton frame of a cube using toothpicks and clay balls, how many clay balls are needed?', 'options': ['8 clay balls', '6 clay balls', '12 clay balls', '4 clay balls'], 'ans': '8 clay balls', 'hint': 'Each clay ball forms a corner (vertex). A cube has 8 corners.', 'explanation': 'Clay balls represent vertices. Because a cube has 8 vertices, exactly 8 clay balls are required.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Recall that clay balls correspond to the corners (vertices) of the 3D solid.', 'step2': 'Determine the number of corners on a cube (8).', 'step3': 'Conclude that 8 clay balls are needed.'}}, {'id': 'c3_q2', 'q': 'How many toothpicks of equal length are needed to construct the complete skeleton of a cube?', 'options': ['12 toothpicks', '8 toothpicks', '6 toothpicks', '10 toothpicks'], 'ans': '12 toothpicks', 'hint': 'Toothpicks represent edges. A cube has 12 edges.', 'explanation': 'Toothpicks represent edges. A cube has 12 edges, so 12 identical toothpicks are needed.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Recall that toothpicks correspond to the straight edges of the shape.', 'step2': 'Count the edges of a cube: 4 at the base, 4 vertical pillars, 4 at the top = 12.', 'step3': 'Conclude that 12 toothpicks are required.'}}, {'id': 'c3_q3', 'q': 'What is the constant sum of numbers on opposite faces of any standard die?', 'options': ['7', '6', '10', '12'], 'ans': '7', 'hint': 'Pair the numbers: 1 and 6, 2 and 5, 3 and 4.', 'explanation': 'Standard dice are manufactured so that opposite faces always add up to exactly 7.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Recall the universal standard dice dot rule.', 'step2': 'Check pairs: 1+6=7, 2+5=7, 3+4=7.', 'step3': 'Confirm that the constant opposite sum is 7.'}}, {'id': 'c3_q4', 'q': 'If a standard die shows 3 dots on top, how many dots are on the bottom face touching the table?', 'options': ['4 dots', '2 dots', '5 dots', '1 dot'], 'ans': '4 dots', 'hint': 'Subtract 3 from the total sum of 7.', 'explanation': 'Opposite faces sum to 7. Therefore, 7 - 3 = 4 dots on the opposite bottom face.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Identify the top face number (3).', 'step2': 'Apply the rule: Top + Bottom = 7.', 'step3': 'Compute Bottom = 7 - 3 = 4 dots.'}}, {'id': 'c3_q5', 'q': 'If a standard die shows 1 dot on its front face, what number is on the hidden back face?', 'options': ['6 dots', '5 dots', '4 dots', '3 dots'], 'ans': '6 dots', 'hint': 'Subtract 1 from 7.', 'explanation': 'Opposite faces sum to 7: 7 - 1 = 6 dots on the back face.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Identify the visible face: 1 dot.', 'step2': 'Subtract from 7: 7 - 1 = 6.', 'step3': 'The opposite back face has 6 dots.'}}, {'id': 'c3_q6', 'q': 'If the bottom face of a standard die has 5 dots, what number is facing upwards?', 'options': ['2 dots', '3 dots', '1 dot', '4 dots'], 'ans': '2 dots', 'hint': 'What number added to 5 makes 7?', 'explanation': 'Because opposite faces add up to 7, the top face must show 7 - 5 = 2 dots.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Given bottom face = 5 dots.', 'step2': 'Calculate top face = 7 - 5 = 2 dots.', 'step3': 'Select 2 dots as the answer.'}}, {'id': 'c3_q7', 'q': 'To build a skeleton frame of a cuboid, how many sets of different length sticks are typically needed?', 'options': ['3 sets of 4 sticks each (length, width, height)', '12 sticks of completely random lengths', 'Only 1 stick length for all 12 edges', '2 sticks total'], 'ans': '3 sets of 4 sticks each (length, width, height)', 'hint': 'A cuboid has 3 dimensions: length, width, and height, each with 4 parallel edges.', 'explanation': 'A general cuboid has 3 dimensions (length, width, height), each requiring 4 parallel equal sticks (4 + 4 + 4 = 12).', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'A cuboid has 12 edges divided into 3 sets of 4 parallel edges.', 'step2': 'Each dimension (length, width, height) has 4 identical edges.', 'step3': 'Conclude that 3 sets of 4 sticks each are needed.'}}, {'id': 'c3_q8', 'q': 'Can you build a skeleton frame of a sphere using straight toothpicks and clay balls?', 'options': ['No, because a sphere has no straight edges or corner points', 'Yes, by using 100 toothpicks in a straight line', 'Yes, a sphere needs exactly 4 toothpicks', 'Yes, using 1 toothpick bent in half'], 'ans': 'No, because a sphere has no straight edges or corner points', 'hint': 'Skeleton models require straight edges and sharp vertices.', 'explanation': 'A sphere has a continuous curved surface with zero straight edges and zero corners, so it cannot be built from sticks and vertex balls.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Recall that stick skeletons represent straight edges meeting at sharp vertices.', 'step2': 'A sphere has 0 straight edges and 0 vertices.', 'step3': 'Conclude that a sphere cannot be built with straight sticks and vertex balls.'}}, {'id': 'c3_q9', 'q': 'In the Toy Joy board game, if your token is on tile 14 and you roll a 5, which tile do you land on?', 'options': ['Tile 19', 'Tile 18', 'Tile 20', 'Tile 17'], 'ans': 'Tile 19', 'hint': 'Add the die roll to your current position: 14 + 5.', 'explanation': 'Starting at tile 14 and moving forward 5 spaces lands you on tile 14 + 5 = 19.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Identify current position: 14.', 'step2': 'Add the rolled distance: 14 + 5.', 'step3': 'The token lands on tile 19.'}}, {'id': 'c3_q10', 'q': 'On a standard die, which two numbers are placed opposite each other?', 'options': ['2 and 5', '1 and 5', '3 and 6', '4 and 2'], 'ans': '2 and 5', 'hint': 'Check which pair adds up to 7.', 'explanation': '2 + 5 = 7, making them an opposite face pair on standard dice.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Check the sums of each pair: 2+5=7, 1+5=6, 3+6=9, 4+2=6.', 'step2': 'Only the pair 2 and 5 sums to 7.', 'step3': 'Conclude that 2 and 5 are placed on opposite faces.'}}]},
    {'id': 20, 'title': 'Challenge 4', 'badge': 'Composite Stacks', 'description': 'Solve advanced multi-cube spatial puzzles, calculate joined face counts, and master hidden block deductions.', 'questions': [{'id': 'c4_q1', 'q': 'When two identical cubes are joined side-by-side along one face, what new shape is formed?', 'options': ['A rectangular cuboid', 'A single larger cube', 'A round cylinder', 'A sphere'], 'ans': 'A rectangular cuboid', 'hint': 'The length doubles while width and height stay the same.', 'explanation': 'Because the length is doubled while width and height remain unchanged, the resulting solid is a cuboid.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Combine two cubes of side length 1.', 'step2': 'Dimensions become length = 2, width = 1, height = 1.', 'step3': 'Since dimensions are not all equal, it is a cuboid.'}}, {'id': 'c4_q2', 'q': 'Two cubes each have 6 faces. When joined together face-to-face, how many total exposed outer faces does the new cuboid have?', 'options': ['10 unit square faces (or 6 cuboid faces)', '12 unit square faces', '8 unit square faces', '6 unit square faces total'], 'ans': '10 unit square faces (or 6 cuboid faces)', 'hint': 'Two faces touch inside the seam and disappear: 6 + 6 - 2 = 10.', 'explanation': 'Before joining, there are 6 + 6 = 12 square faces. Joining hides 2 faces in the seam: 12 - 2 = 10 exposed unit square faces.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Total individual faces before joining = 6 + 6 = 12.', 'step2': 'Touching faces hidden inside the seam = 2.', 'step3': 'Exposed outer square faces = 12 - 2 = 10.'}}, {'id': 'c4_q3', 'q': 'If three unit cubes are joined in a single straight line, what are the dimensions of the resulting cuboid?', 'options': ['Length 3, Width 1, Height 1', 'Length 3, Width 3, Height 3', 'Length 1, Width 1, Height 1', 'Length 2, Width 2, Height 1'], 'ans': 'Length 3, Width 1, Height 1', 'hint': 'Three unit blocks placed end-to-end triple the length.', 'explanation': 'Three unit cubes placed in a line make a solid of length 3 units, width 1 unit, and height 1 unit.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Place 3 unit cubes of side 1 end-to-end.', 'step2': 'Length = 1 + 1 + 1 = 3; width = 1; height = 1.', 'step3': "Select 'Length 3, Width 1, Height 1'."}}, {'id': 'c4_q4', 'q': 'A staircase is built using 3 columns of cubes: column 1 has 1 cube, column 2 has 2 cubes, and column 3 has 3 cubes. How many cubes were used?', 'options': ['6 cubes', '5 cubes', '8 cubes', '9 cubes'], 'ans': '6 cubes', 'hint': 'Add the cubes in each vertical column: 1 + 2 + 3.', 'explanation': 'Adding the columns: 1 + 2 + 3 = 6 unit cubes in total.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'List the cubes in each column: 1, 2, 3.', 'step2': 'Sum the numbers: 1 + 2 + 3 = 6.', 'step3': 'Conclude that exactly 6 cubes were used.'}}, {'id': 'c4_q5', 'q': 'Why must there be a hidden cube under the top step of a 2-cube high tower?', 'options': ['A block cannot float in empty air and needs a foundation block under it', 'It is made of magnetic glue', 'To hide secret toys inside', 'Because top blocks are always invisible'], 'ans': 'A block cannot float in empty air and needs a foundation block under it', 'hint': 'Gravity requires physical support beneath elevated objects.', 'explanation': 'In physical construction, an elevated block must rest upon a solid foundation block beneath it.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Observe the height of the column (2 cubes high).', 'step2': 'Recognize that the upper block must be supported from below.', 'step3': 'Conclude that a foundation cube exists beneath the visible top block.'}}, {'id': 'c4_q6', 'q': 'How many total unit cubes are in a 4-step staircase with columns of heights 1, 2, 3, and 4 cubes?', 'options': ['10 cubes', '12 cubes', '8 cubes', '15 cubes'], 'ans': '10 cubes', 'hint': 'Add 1 + 2 + 3 + 4.', 'explanation': '1 + 2 + 3 + 4 = 10 cubes in total.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'List the column heights: 1, 2, 3, 4.', 'step2': 'Add them: 1 + 2 = 3; 3 + 3 = 6; 6 + 4 = 10.', 'step3': 'Select 10 cubes.'}}, {'id': 'c4_q7', 'q': 'How many flat faces, curved surfaces, and corners does a cylinder have?', 'options': ['2 flat faces, 1 curved surface, 0 corners', '1 flat face, 2 curved surfaces, 1 corner', '6 flat faces, 0 curved surfaces, 8 corners', '0 flat faces, 1 curved surface, 0 corners'], 'ans': '2 flat faces, 1 curved surface, 0 corners', 'hint': 'Top and bottom are flat circles; side is curved; there are no sharp points.', 'explanation': 'A cylinder has 2 flat circular faces, 1 curved lateral surface, 2 curved edges, and zero corners.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Identify top and bottom circles (2 flat faces).', 'step2': 'Identify the rounded lateral wall (1 curved surface).', 'step3': 'Note that no edges meet at a sharp point, so corners = 0.'}}, {'id': 'c4_q8', 'q': 'Which 3D shape has 1 flat circular face, 1 curved surface, 1 circular edge, and 1 pointed corner (apex)?', 'options': ['A cone', 'A cylinder', 'A sphere', 'A cuboid'], 'ans': 'A cone', 'hint': 'Think of an ice cream cone or birthday hat.', 'explanation': 'A cone has 1 flat circular base, 1 curved surface, 1 circular edge, and 1 vertex (apex).', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Review the properties: 1 circular face, 1 curved surface, 1 apex.', 'step2': 'Match these properties with solid shapes.', 'step3': 'Confirm that this defines a cone.'}}, {'id': 'c4_q9', 'q': 'If you stack three identical cubes vertically in a single tower, how tall is the tower if each cube is 4 cm high?', 'options': ['12 cm', '8 cm', '16 cm', '4 cm'], 'ans': '12 cm', 'hint': 'Multiply 3 cubes by 4 cm each.', 'explanation': 'Three cubes stacked vertically have a combined height of 3 * 4 cm = 12 cm.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Identify the height of each cube (4 cm).', 'step2': 'Multiply by the number of cubes in the stack (3).', 'step3': 'Calculate 3 * 4 cm = 12 cm.'}}, {'id': 'c4_q10', 'q': 'Which shape from the Master Geometry Matrix has zero flat faces, zero straight edges, and zero corners?', 'options': ['A sphere', 'A cube', 'A cylinder', 'A cone'], 'ans': 'A sphere', 'hint': 'It is completely round all over like a cricket ball.', 'explanation': 'A sphere is completely bounded by a single continuous curved surface, having 0 flat faces, 0 edges, and 0 corners.', 'solution_steps': {'step1': 'Look at the master geometry matrix row with all zeros for faces, edges, and corners.', 'step2': 'Identify the completely round solid.', 'step3': 'Confirm the shape is a sphere.'}}]},
]
